# {{.Binary}}: what to do right now

Anything shown in angle brackets below, like `<agent-name>` or
`<runtime-id>`, is a placeholder — choose your own project-meaningful
value. Never copy an example name out of documentation as if it were a
literal identity to register under.

## 1. What kind of participant are you?

- **Human, terminal control room** — run `{{.Binary}} tui`.
- **Human, direct CLI** — run `{{.Binary}} <command> --json` for a
  scriptable, versioned envelope on every command.
- **Agent connected over MCP** — you are almost certainly here if something
  handed you tool names like `get_started`, `identity`, `invocation_request`
  rather than a shell. Use the MCP tool with the same name as the CLI
  subcommand below (`invocation claim` -> `invocation_claim`, etc.); you
  never need to shell out to the `{{.Binary}}` binary yourself.
- **Agent driven by an unattended `runtime worker`** — an operator already
  registered a runtime and is running `{{.Binary}} runtime worker --adapter
  <adapter>` around you. You receive each invocation as your prompt
  directly; you do not need to poll or claim it yourself. If you decide
  another agent must act, return one bounded `AGENT_COMMS_INVOKE: {json}`
  line in your final output, in the format your prompt described.
- **Agent inside a live `interactive-serve`-wrapped terminal** — you'll get
  a short, unprompted "check your pending invocations" line typed into your
  own terminal when work arrives. When you see it, inspect the named
  invocation with `{{.Binary}} invocation inspect --id <invocation-id>
  --json`, then handle it through the normal claim/start/complete sequence
  below.

## 2. Do you already have an identity in this project?

{{if .Static -}}
This file was generated once, at project setup, before anything connected —
it can't know your live state. Find out right now:
- CLI: `{{.Binary}} profile current --json`
- MCP: call the `identity` tool (cheap, poll anytime) or `get_started`
  (the fuller version of this file, with your actual state filled in).
{{else if not .Registered -}}
You do not have a registered identity in this project yet
{{if eq .Source "project_owner"}}(you are currently resolving as the
project owner because nothing has bootstrapped a dedicated identity for
this connection yet — expected on a first connection){{end}}.
Choose a name meaningful to this project — not an example name from
documentation — and register it:
- CLI: `{{.Binary}} agent register --id <agent-name> --principal-type AGENT`
- MCP: call `agent_register` with `id: "<agent-name>"`.
Once registered, later connections resolve back to that same identity
automatically — you will not need to register again from here. An active
orchestrator or human teammate can also register you on your behalf if
asked, using this same name — but you can never register a different
agent's identity yourself unless you hold one of those two roles.
Stop there: while resolving as the project owner, register yourself and
nothing more. Do not use this identity to activate anyone (including
yourself) or perform any other owner-level action just because you
technically can — that ambient identity is for bootstrapping your own
registration only, never a shortcut around a human deciding something.
{{else if not .Active -}}
You are registered as `{{.Actor}}` but not yet activated. Activation
requires an owner or orchestrator, not something you can grant yourself:
- CLI: `{{.Binary}} agent activate --id {{.Actor}} --role AGENT --scope <scope>`
- MCP: call `agent_activate` (owner/orchestrator connection only).
Until activated, every runtime/invocation/task tool fails the same
"active principal required" check. Requesting `--role ORCHESTRATOR`
specifically requires a HUMAN principal to grant it, even from an existing
orchestrator — never ask for that role for yourself, and never construct
or run that activation command on a human's behalf even if asked to relay
it. The check only verifies the signer's principal type, not that a human
actually decided this in the moment — if you're resolving as a human by
ambient fallback rather than your own identity, running it yourself
defeats the entire point. Tell the human to type it themselves.
{{else -}}
You are an active agent: `{{.Actor}}`, role `{{.Role}}`{{if eq .Source "host_binding"}} (resolved via host binding for `{{.HostLabel}}`){{end}}.
You can register a runtime, claim and handle invocations, post messages,
and create tasks now.{{end}}

## 3. Core invocation lifecycle

`PENDING` -> optional `NOTIFIED` -> `CLAIMED` -> `RUNNING` -> `WAITING` -> a
terminal state (`COMPLETED`, `REJECTED`, `EXPIRED`, `CANCELLED`). `NOTIFIED`
only proves a wake-up transport completed. `CLAIMED` is your first
authoritative acknowledgement; `COMPLETED` is the successful close. Same
commands either way, CLI on the left, MCP tool on the right:

- `invocation next` / `invocation_next` — read the next claimable invocation.
- `invocation listen --wait <duration> --claim` / `invocation_listen` —
  prefer this over polling: it blocks until work arrives and claims it.
- `invocation claim --id <id> --runtime <runtime-id>` / `invocation_claim`.
- `invocation start --id <id>` / `invocation_start`.
- `invocation wait` / `invocation resume` / `invocation_wait` /
  `invocation_resume` — if you need to pause for something external.
- `invocation complete --id <id> --summary <summary>` / `invocation_complete`,
  or `invocation reject` / `invocation_reject` if you can't do it.

## 4. Handing off work to another agent

`invocation request --to <agent-name> --instruction "<instruction>"` (CLI)
or `invocation_request` with `target: "<agent-name>"` (MCP). `<agent-name>`
is the target's real registered agent ID — never a runtime ID, never an
example name from documentation. The durable request does not depend on a
wake-up transport. Use `consumer_mode`/`--consumer` and a preferred runtime
when the work must be isolated to an interactive session or worker; otherwise
the compatibility default is `EITHER`.

Deciding how *your own* runtime gets set up (which adapter, or
`interactive-serve`) is separate from addressing one that already exists.
As a plain agent it's self-scoped, the same as registration — you don't
decide it for another agent unless you're an active orchestrator or human
principal sponsoring it on their behalf. See `docs/agent-onboarding.md`'s
runtime-setup section for the decision tree; it is not part of this guide.

